g here it's useful  to ask yourself a number of guiding  questions including what went well in  the past year what did not go so well in  which areas did I improve the most in  which areas do I still need to improve  what were my biggest lessons learned  over the year  you don't need to be too rigid with  exactly how you go about this the goal  is simply to get a general idea of your  recent wins and losses so you can use  this information to create a more  optimized plan for the coming year for  example here's what I wrote when  reflecting on my Hungarian learning for  2022. I managed to learn Hungarian very  regularly throughout 2022 spending some  time with a language at least four or  five days each week also I modify my  practice activities to be mainly input  based as opposed to the output based  activities I'd been doing previously I  mainly prioritize listening to podcasts  and watching YouTube videos though I did  read the occasional book as well my  favorite podcast resource has been  Hungarian with sizzy 2022 was also a  great year for my Hungarian learning  because I had the chance to visit  Budapest and spend an entire month there  during the summer I've been to Budapest  before but this was the first time that  I really felt comfortable using the  language in everyday life I also had a  much easier time speaking with natives  than I've had in previous years and at  one point I even managed to have a  two-hour conversation with my tutor  Petra in terms of takeaways I can think  of three learning almost every day  really pays off  solid input skills are essential in  order to build solid output scales  traveling to the country where a  language is spoken can be a great reward  for hard work as well as a worthy  challenge that can spur future growth  you see creating a short but sweet  synopsis of the previous year's efforts  can help you gain a clear perspective on  what you have to do next  speaking of what you have to do next  let's move on to the second part of our  planning process  Step 2 assess your current language  skills when you're learning a language  what you're actually doing is developing  a network of interconnected skills in  your brain the four most prominent of  these skills are listening speaking  reading and writing to determine which  of the four skills you want to develop  in the coming year you should First Take  stock of your current abilities so you  can get a good idea of your strengths  and weaknesses as a learner this is  easier than it sounds all you have to do  for this step is think of a few things  you're capable of doing in each of the  major scale areas it may also be useful  to highlight what you currently cannot  do in these areas so that you know what  to work on next here's how I recently  evaluated my skills for Hungarian  reading I can read scripts of podcasts  aimed at B2 Learners and I've just begun  reading books aimed at native speakers  listen my ability to understand podcasts  and videos has improved drastically in  particular I'm most comfortable with the  podcast that both CZ and Petra produce  and I'm even managing to understand  YouTube videos on certain complex topics  I cannot understand news broadcasts yet  but my listening ability in general is  much improved over where it was one year  ago  speaking I can hold conversations on  most everyday topics without major  problems I still hesitate and make  mistakes of course but overall I feel  quite comfortable even during longer  conversations  writing I did not focus on building my  writing skills in 2022 so I cannot  really evaluate them at this point  however I plan to begin keeping a Daily  Journal in Hungarian starting in 2024.  not too bad right the point of this step  is to describe your language abilities  in concrete terms so that there's less  guesswork when it comes to the type of  practice you need to build into your new  learning plan  on to step number three  step three visualize yourself one year  from now  the last step was all about objectively  evaluating where your language abilities  are this next step is about emotionally  evaluating where you want your language  abilities to be one full year from now  why bring emotions into a learning plan  well your emotional state strongly  influences how motivated or unmotivated  you are to do something  if we can tie strong positive emotions  to your language goals then you'll be  much more likely to stick with them even  when the going gets tough  we can build this connection between our  emotions and our goals through creating  a visualization more specifically by  visualizing our future self after he or  she has accomplished all the goals that  we are about to set in this plan we can  build this connection between our  emotions and our goals through creating  a visualization more specifically by  visualizing our future self after we  have accomplished all the goals that  we're about to set in this plan I like  to treat this as a kind of meditative  exercise I close my eyes  and try to create a vivid mental picture  of myself at the end of the year doing  all the things I want to be able to do  in my target language  once I've created a sufficiently vivid  mental image I write it down so that I  can refer to it and expand upon it  through the year here's the end of the  year visualization I recently created  for Hungarian 31st of December 2023. I'm  in Budapest drinking a glass of palinkai  my friend's apartment we're having a  party and I'm speaking Hungarian with  the people there everyone I talked to is  amazed at how well I speak Hungarian wow  your pronunciation is incredible one  person says you're so fluent says  another I'm excited because I plan on  spending several months in Budapest in  2024 and it'll surely improve even more  by then the future is bright this kind  of visualization exercise may sound  corny at first but I can assure you that  the results can be quite powerful  especially when you keep the written  version handy and look at it whenever  you're tired stressed or otherwise  unmotivated to learn okay time for step  number four step four establish key  goals and objectives for the next three  months  the previous steps shouldn't have given  you a good idea of where you need and  want to improve your language skills by  the end of the year it's time now to  take those General ideas and boil them  down to concrete goals to things you  absolutely want to accomplish in the  next 90 days  why 90 days because that's how we're  going to take the whole year and make it  a bit more digestible instead of  thinking about all 365 days at once  we're primarily focused on 90 days at a  time which helps us remain focused in  the short and medium term so ask  yourself based upon your previous year's  learning your current level and your  visualization what you want to have  accomplished by the end of the next  three months your answer can be anything  so long as it leads you towards the  version of yourself you just pictured in  your visualization here are the language  goals that I want to accomplish about  the end of March 2023. I want to be able  to understand most podcasts aimed at  Learners especially cz's podcast even  the most difficult ones  I want to be able to understand YouTube  videos to a good 60 to 70 percent  without subtitles be it content for  Hungarian Learners or for Native  Hungarian speakers  I want to be able to understand at least  a good 60 to 70 percent of the news  without subtitles I want to be able to  greatly improve my reading skills  especially when it comes to reading  books be it non-fiction or novels I want  to improve my spoken fluency to a level  where I feel comfortable speaking both  with my teacher and Native Hungarian  speakers in general step five break down  your key goals into daily habits  goals are useful for providing a Finish  Line but do little to help you manage  the day-to-day work of language learning  you just created your three month goals  but the key now is to take those goals  and identify the daily actions that will  help you reach them these daily actions  will constitute your habits any habits  will suffice here so long as it consists  of something that if done daily will  realistically get you to your  three-month plan  in the last section I mentioned that I  want to be able to listen to a certain  level of podcasts to watch a certain  level of YouTube video without  difficulty and even watch the news  comfortably to do that I'll naturally  have to spend daily time consuming more  podcasts more videos and News until I  reach that level here's a list of a few  habits that come directly from the goals  I want to accomplish by the end of March  2023. I want to listen to at least one  episode of the Hungarian with sizzy  podcast every day and even take her  Course once it's available  I want to watch at least three YouTube  videos every week I want to watch the  Hungarian news for at least five minutes  a day I want to read at the very least  two or three pages of a book every week  I want to talk to my Hungarian tutor  Petra at least once a week and review  the lesson 24 hours later step 6 review  and adjust your plan every three months  the last step in our six step planning  sequence is actually a recurring one  every three months you should take out  your learning plan to review and revise  it Additionally you should revisit steps  four and five and come up with both  goals and habits for the next three  months  naturally since the year can be divided  up into four periods of 90 days you'll  have to come up with the four 90-day  plans before the year is done so keep  that in mind since it's only January and  this is something you'll have to do  regularly throughout the year I  recommend using a calendar or setting up  some kind of recurring reminder for you  to return to your plan every three  months and set up your new goals and  habits  all right those are the steps of my six  part annual language learning plan  sitting down every January to plan my  language learning like this has done  wonders for my ability to stay focused  and make serious progress in recent  years so I'm certain that it will create  powerful results for you as well let's  quickly recap the six steps of the  planning process reflect on your  language learning progress over the past  year  assess your current language skills  visualize yourself one year from now  establish key goals and objectives for  the next three months  break down your key goals into daily  habits  review and adjust your plan every three  months before we go I want to leave you  with one final tip once you write out  your yearly plan print it out decorate  it and leave it somewhere where you  always see it when you sit down to learn  this will serve you as a powerful  reminder of why you're learning and also  help you remember to review and revise  the plan as the year goes on if you'd  like to learn more about how I plan my  own language learning check the links in  the description box as I've left some  goodies for you there in any case thanks  for watching I wish you all the best as  you begin this new year and I sincerely  hope you'll be able to achieve all of  your language goals by Year's End  see you next time until then Happy  language learning
 

